<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Arrange the Chairs. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Arrange the Chairs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create short descriptions of favorite characters from books. Interactive: Draft questions for guest speakers, including imaginary "guests" or "characters" of relevance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can describe a character involved in classroom tasks (e.g., "My favorite character is Super Tidy. He always helps organize the desks and sweep the floor."). Can write simple questions for guest speakers (e.g., "How can we keep our classroom clean?"). Can share their character description with the class (e.g., "Super Tidy likes to clean. He likes to sweep the floor and arrange the desks.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher writes "Classroom Clean-Up Checklist" on the board. Students work in pairs to quickly list 3-5 classroom tasks they do to keep the room clean, using vocabulary from previous lessons. They write their lists on small papers. Examples: "sweep the floor," "arrange the chairs," "wipe the desks."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple sentences describing a cleaning task. For example, the teacher points to a real mop and says, "This is a mop. I use it to sweep the floor. We are sweeping the floor now." The teacher writes "We are sweeping the floor" on the board, underlining "sweeping." Then, the teacher says, "Sweep the floor, please." and writes it, emphasizing "Sweep." The teacher also models writing, "I can sweep the floor. Can you help me wipe down the desks?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What am I doing right now?" (while pretending to sweep). Expected answer: "You are sweeping."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sweep the floor' an instruction or a question?" Expected answer: "An instruction."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short written description of an imaginary classroom helper character and a few questions for them. Genre: Short narrative/description and questions. Register: Informal and encouraging. Format: Written text on a handout or projected on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Meet Tidy Tom! He is a super helper for our classroom. He is always busy. Right now, he is arranging the chairs carefully and wiping the desks quickly. Tom can clean the whiteboard very well. He says, "Organize the cleaning supplies, please!"</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Questions for Tidy Tom:</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>1. How can we keep our classroom clean every day?</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>2. Can you help us put away the books?</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand and interpret simple written descriptions about classroom tasks and helper characters, recognizing key details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text independently. Then, the teacher asks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "Who is Tidy Tom?" (Expected: A classroom helper.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "Tidy Tom is sweeping the floor." (Expected: False. He is arranging chairs and wiping desks.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: "What can Tidy Tom clean very well?" (Expected: The whiteboard.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using present continuous for ongoing actions, imperatives for instructions, and modal "can" for ability/questions, incorporating vocabulary related to classroom cleaning. Pronunciation focus: Teacher models /r/ in "arrange" and /s/ in "supplies" when writing words on the board, and reviews compound words like "whiteboard" for syllable division.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences about classroom tasks, leaving blanks for target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives) and grammar structures (present continuous, imperatives, can). Students fill in the blanks.
                        Example: "The classroom is very ____ (messy/clean). We ____ (need to / are) ____ (sweep / sweeping) the floor. ____ (Arrange/Can) the chairs, please!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice – Guided Sentence Writing:</b> Students are shown pictures of different classroom tasks (e.g., a student wiping a window, a student putting away books). For each picture, they write two sentences: one using the present continuous to describe the action, and one using an imperative or "can" to give an instruction or state an ability.
                        Example (Picture of a student wiping a window): "She is wiping the window carefully. Wipe the window, please!" or "She can wipe the window."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – Question Drafting:</b> In pairs, students receive a prompt (e.g., "Imagine a cleaning robot visits our class."). They collaboratively draft 2-3 simple questions they would ask this "guest speaker" about keeping the classroom clean and tidy, using "How can we...?" or "Can you help...?" They write their questions on a shared paper. Sociolinguistic aspect: Students are encouraged to use "please" in their questions where appropriate, modeling polite interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product and sharing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "My Classroom Helper Character and Questions"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will create their own imaginary "Classroom Helper" character (e.g., Super Tidy, Captain Clean). They will write a short description (3-5 sentences) of their character, focusing on what the character does to keep the classroom clean and tidy. They must use at least one sentence with the present continuous, one imperative, and one sentence with "can." They should include vocabulary like 'sweep,' 'wipe,' 'arrange,' 'desk,' 'chair,' 'floor,' 'tidy,' 'clean,' 'organized.' After describing their character, they will draft 2-3 simple questions they would ask their character or another classroom helper about maintaining cleanliness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example: "My favorite character is Super Tidy. He is always helping. He is sweeping the floor now. Organize the books, please! He can wipe the whiteboard quickly. How can he always be so tidy?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will draw their character next to their writing. They then share their character description and questions with a partner or small group, reading their written work aloud. Pragmatic focus: Encourage students to use "please" when writing instructions or questions to demonstrate politeness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during production and quick review of written products.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written "My Classroom Helper Character and Questions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses present continuous correctly (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives correctly (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal "can" (affirmative/interrogative) correctly (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., chair, desk, sweep, wipe, tidy) (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Description and questions are clear and relevant to the theme (1 point).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a sentence about cleaning using (circle one: is/are sweeping or sweep!)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing your character description and questions was easy for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and how can you practice it more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., students were able to correctly use imperatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve students' writing accuracy or confidence (e.g., more practice with sentence structure for questions, clearer vocabulary definitions)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students consolidating information and perhaps presenting their written character descriptions or discussing the questions they drafted, using them as a basis for a collaborative 21st Century Skills Project related to classroom cleanliness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>